                <div style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                    <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Understanding SPI</h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                        SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface) is a high-speed synchronous communication protocol 
                        that uses four wires and supports full-duplex communication between master and slave devices.
                    </p>
                    
                    <div class="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4">
                        <h4 class="font-semibold text-green-800 mb-2">SPI Signal Lines:</h4>
                        <ul class="text-green-700 space-y-1">
                            <li><strong>MOSI (Master Out Slave In):</strong> Data from master to slave</li>
                            <li><strong>MISO (Master In Slave Out):</strong> Data from slave to master</li>
                            <li><strong>SCK (Serial Clock):</strong> Clock signal from master</li>
                            <li><strong>CS/SS (Chip Select/Slave Select):</strong> Selects which slave to communicate with</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    
                    <div class="bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h4 class="font-semibold text-yellow-800 mb-2">SPI vs I2C Comparison:</h4>
